<%# A real block, planned against lifted, readable without an account. #693.

    Show HN asks for something people can look at "without barriers such as signups", and this
    is the page every launch post links to: what the app does, shown on four real weeks rather
    than described. The data is lib/tectonic/example_block.rb, a frozen snapshot of the owner's
    Block 0, so nothing here reads an account.

    It keeps the parts that did not go to plan -- skipped squat days, unfinished deadlift sets,
    a bench that went over -- because the claim this page makes is that the plan is checked
    against what was lifted, and a block where nothing diverged would not show that happening.
    Plates are left out; the snapshot's comment says why. %>
<div class="<%= page_style(:reading) %>">
  <h1 class="serif text-4xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Four weeks, planned and lifted</h1>

  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-700">
    One lifter&rsquo;s real training block, written by an AI assistant through the tectonic plates
    connector and ticked off set by set at the rack. Each week shows what was planned for the
    three main lifts next to what was actually lifted, including the parts that didn&rsquo;t go
    to plan.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    No account needed to read this. Weights are in pounds; RPE is how hard a set felt, out of 10.
    Names and dates are left out.
  </p>

  <%- Tectonic::ExampleBlock::WEEKS.each do |week| %>
    <section class="mt-10" aria-labelledby="week-<%= week.number %>">
      <h2 id="week-<%= week.number %>" class="<%= heading_style(:section) %>">
        Week <%= week.number %>: <%= week.title %>
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm italic text-gray-600">The plan&rsquo;s note: &ldquo;<%= week.note %>&rdquo;</p>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="text-gray-500">
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Lift</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Planned</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-medium">Lifted</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100 text-gray-800">
            <%- week.lifts.each do |lift| %>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-gray-900"><%= lift.name %></th>
                <td class="py-2 pr-4"><%= lift.plan %></td>
                <td class="py-2">
                  <%- unless lift.sets.empty? %>
                    <%= lift.sets.map { |set| Tectonic::ExampleBlock.set_text(set) }.join(', ') %>
                  <%- end %>
                  <%- if lift.not_done %>
                    <span class="text-gray-500">(<%= lift.not_done %> sets not done)</span>
                  <%- end %>
                  <%- if lift.note %>
                    <p class="text-gray-600"><%= lift.note %></p>
                  <%- end %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <%- end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <%- if week.also %>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700"><%= week.also %></p>
      <%- end %>
    </section>
  <%- end %>

  <h2 class="<%= heading_style(:section) %> mt-12">What you&rsquo;re looking at</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
    Weeks 2 to 4 are written as percentages of a training max, so the assistant could raise or
    hold them after reading how week 1 went. Every set above was logged against its plan, which
    is what lets the next block start from what was actually lifted rather than what was
    written down.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3">
    <a href="/create-account" class="<%= primary_button_style %>">Create an account</a>
    <a href="/docs" class="text-sm font-semibold leading-6 text-sky-800">How to connect your assistant <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
  </p>
</div>
